==============================================================================
C3  decomposition counterexample under C2
==============================================================================
  h(0.2) = 0.169327146748297   h(0.6) = 0.616099208134857
  pure p=0.4           : 0.383900791865143   (spec 0.38390)
  50/50 mix of 0.2,0.6 : 0.392713177441577   (spec 0.39271)
  difference           : 0.008812385576
  Under C1 (h=p): pure 0.4, mixture (0.2+0.6)/2 = 0.4 exactly.

  NOTE: pure |psi> with p=0.4 and the 50/50 mixture of pure p=0.2, p=0.6 states have DIFFERENT
  density matrices (off-diagonals).  Same-rho comparisons (Bloch vector r=(0,0,-0.2), rho=diag(0.4,0.6)):
   A {p=1,p=0} w=.4,.6              rho_11=0.4 |rho_01|=0.0  outcome-1 freq = 0.4
   B {p=.4 phi=0, p=.4 phi=pi}      rho_11=0.4 |rho_01|=1.01e-43  outcome-1 freq = 0.3839007919
   C {.2/0,.2/pi,.6/0,.6/pi}        rho_11=0.4 |rho_01|=9.2e-44  outcome-1 freq = 0.3927131774
  => same rho, three different frequencies under C2: rho-dependence also violated.

==============================================================================
HOSTILE side-checks
==============================================================================
(H1) interior zero of sigma, b = 0:  sigma(p) = 4 sqrt(kappa) p(1-p)|2p-1|
   int_(1/4)^(1/2-1e-2) dx/sigma^2 = 24.55864227
   int_(1/4)^(1/2-1e-4) dx/sigma^2 = 2499.578446
   int_(1/4)^(1/2-1e-8) dx/sigma^2 = 24999999.58
   -> diverges like 1/(4 (1/2-x)): 1/sigma^2 not locally integrable at 1/2 => 1/2 is an absorbing (natural, attracting) trap
   started at p<1/2: p_t bounded martingale on [0,1/2], converges to 0 or 1/2,
   P(->1/2) = 2p, P(->1) = 0.  So h(p) = 0 on (0,1/2], h(p) = 2p-1 on [1/2,1): martingale but NOT Born.
   e.g. h(0.4) = 0 vs Born 0.4;  50/50 mix of {0,1}: 0.5 vs h(0.5) = 0  -> decomposition-dependent.

(H2) both endpoints non-attracting (entrance): sigma^2=2p(1-p), b=(1/2-p)*c with c=3 (b set to 0 at endpoints)
[H2] s'(x) = 1/(8*(x*(1 - x))**(3/2)),   m(x) = 8*sqrt(x*(1 - x))
   eps=1e-4 : S=24.707575    M=0.61417952   Sigma=14.977385    N=0.19750167
   eps=1e-8 : S=2499.7113    M=0.61418485   Sigma=1535.0871    N=0.19770009
   eps=1e-16: S=25000000.0   M=0.61418485   Sigma=15354621.0   N=0.19770011
   => boundary 0 is entrance (inaccessible, non-attracting)
   s(0+)=-inf, s(1-)=+inf: recurrent in (0,1); p_t never converges => h = 0 on (0,1), h(1)=1.
   h is constant on (0,1): interior decompositions give p-bar-independent value 0, yet h != p and b != 0.
   With decompositions allowed to use the endpoints: 0.5 h(0)+0.5 h(1) = 0.5 != h(0.5) = 0, detected.

(H3) b nonzero on a Lebesgue-null set N (e.g. b = 1 on rationals): occupation-time formula
   int_0^t 1_N(p_s) ds = int 1_N(a) L_t^a / sigma^2(a) da = 0, so the drift integral vanishes:
   law identical to b=0, h = p, p_t martingale, yet b != 0 pointwise.  Only b=0 a.e. is determined.
